ut 112.321 seconds (1 minute 52.321 seconds) each.\n\nBut we may do approximate splits: part 1: 0:00-1:52, part 2: 1:52-3:44, part 3: 3:44-5:36, part 4: 5:36-7:28, part 5: 7:28-9:20.\n\nWe need to map each cue to a part based on start time. Then pick approx 4 phrases per part (20/5 =4). However there's rule: each part should have about a fifth of count, but up to 35% of count per part. We can aim for 4 phrases per part. If insufficient, we can borrow from neighbors.\n\nNow we need to identify eligible cues.\n\nFirst, filter out non-useful lines. Let's list all cues and mark suitability.\n\nWe need to decide which lines are likely best for intermediate Spanish. We'll go through each cue and annotate if it's candidate.\n\nCue #1: \"Subtítulos: Equipo Paraguas\" – caption line, not a phrase for learning. Discard.\n\nCue #2: \"¡Buenos días! ¿Qué te pongo?\" – Good phrase: a greeting and request \"Good morning! What can I get you?\" That's useful. Candidate.\n\nCue #3: \"Un café con leche, por favor.\" – Useful phrase for ordering coffee. Candidate.\n\nCue #4: \"¿Para aquí o para llevar?\" – \"For here or to take away?\" Useful ordering phrase. Candidate.\n\nCue #5: \"Para llevar. Tengo un poco de prisa.\" – Answer, but includes \"I have a little hurry\" maybe less common. Could be candidate. Usage: giving reason for takeaway.\n\nCue #6: \"Claro. ¿Algo más?\" – \"Sure. Anything else?\" Useful phrase. Candidate.\n\nCue #7: \"No, gracias. ¿Cuánto es?\" – \"No, thank you. How much is it?\" Good for transactions. Candidate.\n\nCue #8: \"Son dos euros con cincuenta.\" – \"It's two euros fifty.\" Useful for telling price. Candidate.\n\nCue #9: \"¿Puedo pagar con tarjeta?\" – \"Can I pay by card?\" Good phrase. Candidate.\n\nCue #10: \"Sí, sin problema.\" – \"Yes, no problem.\" Useful.\n\nCue #11: \"MARCO: ¡Lena! ¿Qué haces por aquí?\" – Speaker label with exclamation. The phrase \"¡Lena! ¿Qué haces por aquí?\" is a greeting and question, maybe candidate. We can keep text after