:12.965 = 432.965 sec. #114 7:16.910 = 436.910 sec. #115 7:20.225 = 440.225 sec. #116 7:24.085 = 444.085 sec. #117 7:27.425 = 447.425 sec. #118 7:32.335 = 452.335 sec > 449.284, so #118 belongs to part5. So part4 includes cues #88-#117 inclusive.\n\nNow part5: from about 7:29.30 (449.284 sec) to end (approx 559 seconds). Cues #118 onward (and maybe earlier some on the cusp near 7:32). So part5 includes cues #118-#146.\n\nNow we have 5 parts with candidate cues.\n\nNow we need to pick about 4 phrases per part (20 total). We'll need to apply selection criteria: level intermediate, everyday usefulness, not redundant with earlier sheet (none). We must avoid names only lines, filler, sound/lyrics, credit, etc.\n\nWe'll have many suitable candidate lines in each part. Let's list eligible candidate cues per part:\n\n### Part 1 (cues #2-#30)\nActually exclude #1 credit. We'll list all #2-#30, but we need to filter out some that may be not suitable: we have #2 through #30 all okay, except maybe #31 is not in part1. So we need to pick 4 from part1.\n\nPotential candidates for part 1 (with details):\n- #2 \"¡Buenos días! ¿Qué te pongo?\" (Greeting, ask)\n- #3 \"Un café con leche, por favor.\"\n- #4 \"¿Para aquí o para llevar?\"\n- #5 \"Para llevar. Tengo un poco de prisa.\"\n- #6 \"Claro. ¿Algo más?\"\n- #7 \"No, gracias. ¿Cuánto es?\"\n- #8 \"Son dos euros con cincuenta.\"\n- #9 \"¿Puedo pagar con tarjeta?\"\n- #10 \"Sí, sin problema.\"\n- #11 \"¡Lena! ¿Qué haces por aquí?\" (after speaker removal: \"¡Lena! ¿Qué haces por aquí?\" maybe okay)\n- #12 \"¡Marco! Qué casualidad.\"\n- #13 \"¿Ya te has mudado?\"\n- #14 \"Todavía no. Sigo buscando piso.\"\n- #15 \"¿En serio? ¿Y cómo va la búsqueda?\"\n- #16 \"Regular. Todo es carísimo.\"\n- #17 \"Te entiendo perfectamente.\"\n- #18 \"Hoy tengo una visita a las once.\"\n- #19 \"¿Quieres que te acompañe?\"\n- #20 \"No hace falta, pero gracias.\"\n- #21 \"Oye, va a llover. ¿Tienes paraguas?\"\n- #22 \"No, se me olvidó en casa.\"\n- #23 \"Toma, llévate el mío.\"\n- #24 \"¿Estás seguro?\"\n- #25 \"Segurísimo. Me lo devuelves esta noche.\"\n- #26 \"Vale. Te debo una.\"\n- #27 \"No te preocupes. ¿Cenamos juntos a las nueve?\"\n- #28 \"Me parece genial.\"\n- #29 \"Perfecto. ¡Hasta luego!\"\n- #30 \"Aquí tienes tu café.\"\n\nThat's a lot. We can choose a varied set: maybe include greeting, ordering, payment, weather, small talk, etc.\n\nCandidate picks (4):\n1. #2 \"¡Buenos días! ¿Qué te pongo?\" – common greeting in cafés.\n2. #3 \"Un café con leche, por favor.\" – ordering.\n3. #4 \"¿Para aquí o para llevar?\" – ask for dine-in or takeaway.\n4. #9 \"¿Puedo pagar con tarjeta?\" – payment method.\n\nAlternatively could include #21 \"Oye, va a llover. ¿Tienes paraguas?\" – good for small talk about weather. But as we need 4, we could shift.\n\nSince we also have other parts that also have such weather lines, could distribute.\n\nMaybe part1 picks these 4: #2, #3, #4, #9. That's good.\n\nCheck for context lines: before and after. We need to capture line before and after each phrase (the cue preceding and following). We'll need to store those.\n\nFor #2: before: could be earlier cue #1 but that's \"Subtítulos: Equipo Paraguas\". That's not ideal. But okay, we must follow rule: line before exactly from subtitle. Since #1 is credit line, might be okay. After: #3 line text.\n\nBut maybe better pick a phrase that has more natural context lines (i.e., not a credit line). However the rules don't prohibit using credit lines as context, but maybe not essential. It will be displayed.\n\nAlternatively pick #5 \"Para llevar. Tengo un poco de prisa.\" context before is #4, after is #6.\n\nCheck #8 \"Son dos euros con cincuenta.\" context before #7 \"No, gracias. ¿Cuánto es?\" after #9 \"¿Puedo pagar con tarjeta?\" That also forms nice context.\n\nLet's decide.\n\nWe can pick better four that are well distributed.\n\nMaybe choose:\n- #2 \"¡Buenos días! ¿Qué te pongo?\" (café greeting)\n- #5 \"Para llevar. Tengo un poco