 --> 00:05:10,800 Of course. Will you tell me something tomorrow?\n76 00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:15,160 Yes, I'll write to you tomorrow without fail.\n77 00:05:15,860 --> 00:05:18,745 Very good. Oh, look how it's raining!\n78 00:05:19,695 --> 00:05:22,690 Less bad that I have an umbrella.\n79 00:05:23,490 --> 00:05:25,690 (Laughter)\n80 00:05:26,290 --> 00:05:28,290 Aha.\n81 00:05:30,890 --> 00:05:34,435 Good afternoon. How can I help you?\n82 00:05:35,135 --> 00:05:37,910 I'm not feeling very well.\n83 00:05:38,860 --> 00:05:40,975 What's wrong?\n84 00:05:42,175 --> 00:05:45,830 My throat hurts and I'm very tired.\n85 00:05:46,530 --> 00:05:48,700 Since when?\n86 00:05:49,650 --> 00:05:52,370 Since last night.\n87 00:05:53,570 --> 00:05:55,740 Do you have a fever?\n88 00:05:56,440 --> 00:05:59,710 I don't think so, but I'm not sure.\n89 00:06:00,660 --> 00:06:04,645 I recommend these throat lozenges.\n90 00:06:05,845 --> 00:06:08,730 Take one every four hours.\n91 00:06:09,430 --> 00:06:12,920 Can I take them on an empty stomach?\n92 00:06:13,870 --> 00:06:16,535 Better after eating.\n93 00:06:17,735 --> 00:06:20,125 And drink plenty of water.\n94 00:06:20,825 --> 00:06:23,105 And if I don't get better?\n95 00:06:24,055 --> 00:06:27,765 If you don't improve in three days, go to the doctor.\n96 00:06:28,965 --> 00:06:31,905 All right. How much do I owe you?\n97 00:06:32,605 --> 00:06:35,215 Six euros twenty.\n98 00:06:36,165 --> 00:06:38,170 Here you are.\n99 00:06:39,370 --> 00:06:41,540 Get well soon.\n100 00:06:42,240 --> 00:06:44,740 Thank you, that's very kind.\n101 00:06:52,690 --> 00:06:55,850 Finally! I thought you weren't coming.\n102 00:06:57,050 --> 00:07:00,100 Sorry, I'm late again.\n103 00:07:00,800 --> 00:07:04,015 Don't worry. I've just arrived.\n104 00:07:04,965 --> 00:07:08,620 Here's your umbrella. It saved my life.\n105 00:07:09,820 --> 00:07:12,265 How did the viewing go?\n106 00:07:12,965 --> 00:07:15,960 Very well. I love the flat.\n107 00:07:16,910 --> 00:07:19,025 And the price?\n108 00:07:20,225 --> 00:07:23,385 A bit expensive, but it's worth it.\n109 00:07:24,085 --> 00:07:26,475 Are you going to take it?\n110 00:07:27,425 --> 00:07:31,135 I think so. I'll write to the owner tomorrow.\n111 00:07:32,335 --> 00:07:35,550 Congratulations! We have to celebrate.\n112 00:07:36,250 --> 00:07:38,585 What will you have?\n113 00:07:39,535 --> 00:07:42,035 What do you recommend?\n114 00:07:43,235 --> 00:07:46,230 The paella is very good today.\n115 00:07:46,930 --> 00:07:49,870 Then two paellas, please.\n116 00:07:50,820 --> 00:07:53,870 And a bottle of still water.\n117 00:07:55,070 --> 00:07:57,020 Right away.\n118 00:07:57,720 --> 00:08:01,045 Hey, are you OK? You don't look well.\n119 00:08:01,995 --> 00:08:05,265 I've got a bit of a cold, that's all.\n120 00:08:06,465 --> 00:08:09,955 You should rest this weekend.\n121 00:08:10,655 --> 00:08:14,530 You're right, but I have a thousand things to do.\n122 00:08:15,480 --> 00:08:18,695 Shall I give you a hand with the move?\n123 00:08:19,895 --> 00:08:22,890 Really? That would be great.\n124 00:08:23,590 --> 00:08:26,805 Of course, that's what friends are for.\n125 00:08:27,755 --> 00:08:30,365 I owe you one, again.\n126 00:08:31,565 --> 00:08:34,780 You owe me two. But who's counting?\n127 00:08:35,480 --> 00:08:38,365 What do you mean by that?\n128 00:08:39,315 --> 00:08:42,530 That you invite me to the next dinner.\n129 00:08:43,730 --> 00:08:45,790 Deal made.\n130 00:08:46,490 --> 00:08:49,430 Here you are. Enjoy your meal!\n131 00:08:50,380 --> 00:08:52,715 What a great look!\n132 00:08:53,915 --> 00:08:56,305 For the new flat.\n133 00:08:57,005 --> 00:08:58,790 Cheers!\n134 00:08:59,740 --> 00:09:02,735 By the way, is it still raining?\n135 00:09:03,935 --> 00:09:05,940 It's pouring.\n136 00:09:06,640 --> 00:09:10,350 So you'll lend me the umbrella again.\n137 00:09:11,300 --> 00:09:14,845 No way. This time I'll walk you home.\n138 00:09:16,045 --> 00:09:18,105 You're a sweetheart.\n\nOkay. So we have a clear mapping; the timestamps are mostly similar (e.g., Spanish 00:00:00.500 corresponds to English 00:00:04.000, but alignment likely uses offset perhaps 3.5 seconds. But alignment check says aligned. We can produce times from Spanish file (target). For meaning (translation) we will use English line.\n\nNow we need to select 20 intermediate-level phrases.\n\nWe need to split scene range into five equal parts by time. The first cue start is 00:00:00,500; last cue end likely at 00:09:18,105 for English, but Spanish last is 00:09:16,045 to 00:09:18,105? The Spanish last entry is 00:09:16,045 -> 00:09:18,105 (Eres un sol). So total duration ~ 9:18 - start ~ 0:00:00.5 = approx 9 minutes 18 seconds ~ 558 seconds. Let's compute: 9 minutes = 540 seconds, plus 18 seconds = 558 seconds. Starting offset ~0.5 seconds. But we can treat first cue start as 0:00:00.500, last start about 00:09:16.045. So time range ~ 557.5 seconds.\n\nDividing into 5 equal parts: each ~111.5 seconds (~1:51.5). We'll approximate boundaries:\n\nPart 1: 00:00:00.500 to ~00:01:51. (00:01:51. maybe 00:01:51). That includes cues up to around 00:01:51.\n\nPart 2: 00:01:51 to 00:03:42.\n\nPart 3: 00:03:42 to 00:05:33.\n\nPart 4: 00:05:33 to 00:07:24.\n\nPart 5: 00:07:24 to 00:09:18.\n\nWe need around 4 phrases per part.\n\nWe must apply phrase selection criteria for intermediate level: 3-10 words, everyday usefulness, not proper nouns alone, not filler, etc.\n\nLet's list Spanish cues in each segment and choose appropriate ones.\n\nSegment 1 (0:00:00-1:51) includes lines up to approx cue number about 20 (since 00:01:50). Let's list them with timestamps and texts:\n\n1: 00:00:00,500 - Subtítulos: Equipo Paraguas (title phrase, not a phrase)\n2: 00:00:04,000 - ¡Buenos días! ¿Qué te pongo? (question in a cafe)\n3: 00:00:07,640 - Un café con leche, por favor. (order)\n4: 00:00:11,585 - ¿Para aquí o para llevar? (question about takeout)\n5: 00:00:15,890 - Para llevar. Tengo un poco de prisa. (response)\n6: 00:00:19,970 - Claro. ¿Algo más? (ask for more)\n7: 00:00:23,255 - No, gracias. ¿Cuánto es? (question about price)\n8: 00:00:27,175 - Son dos euros cincuenta. (price)\n9: 00:00:30,815 - ¿Puedo pagar con tarjeta? (payment method)\n10: 00:00:34,540 - Sí, sin problema. (affirmation)\n11: 00:00:38,075 - ¡Lena! ¿Qué haces por aquí? (greeting, name)\n12: 00:00:41,660 - ¡Marco! Qué casualidad. (greeting)\n13: 00:00:45,275 - ¿Ya te has mudado? (question about moving)\n14: 00:00:48,865 - Todavía no. Sigo buscando piso. (response)\n15: 00:00:52,670 - ¿En serio? ¿Y cómo va la búsqueda? (question about house hunt)\n16: 00:00:56,890 - Regular. Todo es carísimo. (comment)\n17: 00:01:00,920 - Te entiendo perfectamente. (empathy)\n18: 00:01:04,450 - Hoy tengo una visita a las once. (inform about schedule)\n19: 00:01:08,560 - ¿Quieres que te acompañe? (offer)\n20: 00:01:12,535 - No hace falta, pero gracias. (decline politely)\n21: 00:01:16,175 - Oye, va a llover. ¿Tienes paraguas? (note about weather + question)\n22: 00:01:20,450 - No, se me olvidó en casa. (negative)\n23: 00:01:24,425 - MARCO: Toma, llévate el mío. (offer umbrella)\n24: 00:01:27,680 - ¿Estás seguro? (question)\n25: 00:01:30,800 - Segurísimo. Me lo devuelves esta noche. (agreement)\n... up to 01:51 includes up to cue = maybe 30 (ends at 01:52?). Let's continue:\n\n26: 00:01:35,545 - Vale. Te debo una. (acknowledge debt)\n27: 00:01:38,635 - No te preocupes. ¿Cenamos juntos a las nueve? (suggest dinner)\n28: 00:01:43,460 - Me parece genial. (positive)\n29: 00:01:46,995 - Perfecto. ¡Hasta luego! (farewell)\n30: 00:01:50,360 - Aquí tienes tu café. (hand coffee)\n31: 00:01:53,810 - Muchas gracias. ¡Que tengas un buen día! (thanks/goodbye)\n32: 00:01:58,210 - ♪ Llueve sobre la ciudad ♪ (song lyric, exclude)\n33: 00:02:01,010 - Mmm. (filler, exclude)\n34: 00:02:05,610 - Perdone, ¿me puede ayudar? (request help)\n...\n\nWe need 4 phrases from part 1 (0:00:00 - 01:51). Let's pick phrases suitable for intermediate learners, with everyday usage.\n\nPotential candidates:\n\n1. \"¡Buenos días! ¿Qué te pongo?\" (Good morning! What can I get you?) - typical cafe greeting.\n\n2. \"Un café con leche, por favor.\" (A coffee with milk, please.) - ordering.\n\n3. \"¿Para aquí o para llevar?\" (For here or to go?) - useful phrase at cafes.\n\n4. \"¿Cuánto es?\" (How much is it?) - ask about price.\n\nThese are all okay. Also \"¿Puedo pagar con tarjeta?\" (Can I pay by card?) is useful, but we have only 4 needed.\n\nWe also consider variation, perhaps include \"¿Quieres que te acompañe?\" (Do you want me to accompany you?) but maybe later in part 2.\n\nNow count the words: \"¡Buenos días! ¿Qué te pongo?\" -> 5 words? \"Buenos días\" (2) \"¿Qué\" (1) \"te\" (1) \"pongo?\" (1). That's fine.\n\n\"Un café con leche, por favor.\" -> 5 words.\n\n\"¿Para aquí o para llevar?\" -> 5 words.\n\n\"¿Cuánto es?\" -> 2 words (maybe too short but still okay; intermediate maybe 2-6 words? It fits beginner levels 2-6; intermediate okay too. It's short but maybe acceptable. Could add \"¿Cuánto cuesta?\" but we have \"¿Cuánto es?\" It's okay.\n\nWe'll choose these as four phrases for part 1.\n\nNow part 2 (01:51 to 03:42). That includes cues roughly from number 33 to 70 maybe.\n\nLet's list Spanish cues after 00:02:05.610 (the start of part 2). We'll enumerate from cue numbers after index.\n\nWe've already at cue ~34. Let's continue:\n\n34 (00:02:05,610) Perdone, ¿me puede ayudar? (Would you help me? polite request)\n35 (00:02:09,140) Claro, dígame. (Sure, go ahead.)\n36 (00:02:12,260) Busco el tren para el centro. (I'm looking for the train to the city centre.)\n37 (00:02:16,455) Sale del andén cuatro. (It leaves from platform four.)\n38 (00:02:19,765) ¿A qué hora sale el próximo? (What time does the next one leave?)\n39 (00:02:23,655) A las diez y cuarto, pero hoy va con retraso. (At quarter past ten, but it's running late today.)\n40 (00:02:28,730) ¿Con retraso? ¿Cuánto tiempo? (Late? How long?)\n41 (00:02:32,425) Unos veinte minutos, más o menos. (About twenty minutes, more or less.)\n42 (00:02:36,590) Vaya. Tengo una cita a las once. (Oh. I have an appointment at eleven.)\n43 (00:02:40,950) Entonces le conviene tomar el autobús. (Then you'd better take the bus.)\n44 (00:02:45,140) ¿Está lejos de aquí la parada? (Is the stop far from here?)\n45 (00:02:49,140) No, está a cinco minutos andando. (No, it's a five-minute walk.)\n46 (00:02:53,555) Salga por la puerta principal y gire a la izquierda. (Exit through main door and turn left.)\n47 (00:02:58,455) ¿Y qué autobús tengo que coger? (Which bus do I need to take?)\n48 (00:03:02,510) El treinta y dos. Pasa cada diez minutos. (The thirty-two. It comes every ten minutes.)\n49 (00:03:07,365) ¿Puede repetirlo, por favor? Más despacio. (Could you repeat that, please? More slowly.)\n50 (00:03:11,775) El treinta y dos, cada diez minutos. (The thirty-two, every ten minutes.)\n51 (00:03:16,105) Perfecto. Muchas gracias por su ayuda. (Perfect. Thank you very much for your help.)\n52 (00:03:20,795) De nada. ¡Buen viaje! (You're welcome. Have a good trip!)\n53 (00:03:24,050) ¿Señora Ortiz? Soy Lena. (Mrs. Ortiz? I'm Lena.)\n54 (00:03:27,720) Lo siento, voy a llegar un poco tarde. (Sorry, I'm going to be a bit late.)\n55 (00:03:32,410) No pasa nada. Te espero en el portal. (No problem. I'll wait for you at the entrance.)\n56 (00:03:36,545) Gracias por su paciencia. (Thank you for your patience.)\n57 (00:03:47,270) Come, come. Este es el salón. (Come in, come in. This is the living room.)\n58 (00:03:51,465) ¡Qué luminoso! (So bright!)\n59 (00:03:54,335) Da al sur, así que tiene sol toda la tarde. (It faces south, so it gets sun all afternoon.)\n60 (00:03:59,050) ¿Cuántas habitaciones tiene? (How many bedrooms does it have?)\n61 (00:04:03,190) Dos habitaciones y un baño. (Two bedrooms and a bathroom.)\n62 (00:04:06,775) ¿Puedo ver la cocina? (Can I see the kitchen?)\n63 (00:04:10,280) Por supuesto. Está recién reformada. (Of course. It's just been renovated.)\n64 (00:04:14,860) ¿Cuánto es el alquiler al mes? (How much is the rent per month?)\n65 (00:04:18,610) Ochocientos euros, gastos incluidos. (Eight hundred euros, bills included.)\n66 (00:04:22,940) ¿Incluye la calefacción? (Does it include heating?)\n67 (00:04:26,860) Sí, la calefacción y el agua. (Yes, heating and water.)\n68 (00:04:30,555) ¿Hay que pagar fianza? (Is there a deposit?)\n69 (00:04:34,115) Dos meses de fianza. (Two months deposit.)\n70 (00:04:37,815) ¿Se admiten mascotas? (Are pets allowed?)\n\nWe'll need four phrases from part 2.\n\nSelect intermediate-level useful phrases:\n\n- \"Busco el tren para el centro.\" (I'm looking for the train to the city centre.) Useful travel phrase.\n\n- \"¿A qué hora sale el próximo?\" (What time does the next one leave?) Useful question for transport.\n\n- \"¿Cuánto tiempo?\" is filler, but maybe \"¿Cuánto tiempo?\" not separate line; the phrase \"¿Con retraso? ¿Cuánto tiempo?\" combine two questions; might be okay but is maybe not best.\n\n- \"¿Cuánto es el alquiler al mes?\" (How much is the rent per month?) Useful for housing.\n\n- \"¿Incluye la calefacción?\" (Does it include heating?) Useful for housing.\n\nWe need 4 phrases. Pick:\n\n1. \"Busco el tren para el centro.\" (train)\n\n2. \"¿A qué hora sale el próximo?\" (time next train)\n\n3. \"¿Cuánto es el alquiler al mes?\" (rent amount)\n\n4. \"¿Se admiten mascotas?\" (are pets allowed?) Actually check phrase \"¿Se admiten mascotas?\" appears later at cue 70. Yes.\n\nAlternatively we could pick \"¿Puedo ver la cocina?\" but okay.\n\nNow part 3 (03:42 to 05:33). That's Spanish cues from around cue ~71 to maybe 100.\n\nWe continue enumerating after 70.\n\n71 (00:04:41,070) \"Depende. ¿Qué mascota tienes?\" (It depends. What pet do you have?)\n\n72 (00:04:45,015) \"Un gato muy tranquilo.\" (A very calm cat.)\n\n73 (00:04:48,825) \"Un gato no es problema.\" (A cat is no problem.)\n\n74 (00:04:52,190) \